*(
    title: Cell Structure
    description: What every cell has in common, and the two fundamentally different ways of building one.
    tags: cells, prokaryote, eukaryote, organelles, module 1, biology, stub
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Every living thing is made of cells, and every cell comes from a cell before it. Nothing in the rest of the subject makes sense until that is settled, because every process biology studies is either happening inside a cell or between cells.

    Written out here: the organelles of a eukaryotic cell and the job each one does, the far simpler prokaryotic cell with no nucleus and no membrane-bound compartments, and why that difference is the deepest division in the living world.

    Three things follow immediately. The instruments and the argument that established cells in the first place are in *Microscopy and Cell Theory*; the boundary deciding what a cell admits is *The Cell Membrane*; and the proteins that make its chemistry possible at all are in *Enzymes*.

    internote(ref: microscopy-and-cell-theory.chalk)

    internote(ref: the-cell-membrane.chalk)

    internote(ref: enzymes.chalk)
}
